explanation:
The Object Recognition Flow currently runs at 473 ms, which exceeds its 430 ms target, and the edge node N4 is already overloaded (12.2 of 9 vCPUs used), so the current setup does not satisfy the updated requirements.

A new configuration has been calculated that meets all targets:
- the Object Recognition service is moved to the cloud node N6 (where 27.5 GB RAM remain free and CPU capacity is ample), ensuring the Object Recognition Flow drops to 430 ms and the service’s 9 vCPU requirement is met;
- the AR Rendering service stays on the edge node N4, where 12.2 of 9 vCPUs are used but the AR Rendering Flow still meets its 501 ms budget (currently 482 ms);
- the Scene Processing service is also placed on N6 to keep scene traffic local to the cloud, freeing up the edge node;
- the Vision Capture and Display Output services remain on their current nodes (N12 and N8 respectively) and continue to meet their own constraints.

With these moves, both latency targets and resource requirements are satisfied.
